  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 937 PM MDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nCHILLY AND DRY WEEK:\n\nNear-zonal flow is established across the Intermountain West, \nmaintaining relatively dry conditions for our CWA. This keeps \nprecipitation chances near-zero for most locations with little \nmore than scattered cloud cover expected. We're sitting near- \nnormal in terms of high temperatures as well, leaving valleys in\nthe 50's to low-60's, and mountain locations in the 40's to \nlow-50's. Though we continue to dry out steadily the next few \ndays, some valley locations may still experience fog around \nsunrise which may impact morning commutes.\n\nNEXT ROUND OF PRECIPITATION THIS WEEKEND:\n\nAs flow turns southwesterly ahead of a PacNW low, moisture \nbegins its return to the region. The first signs of this pattern\nshift are expected on Friday as isolated storms will begin to \npop up, though at this point QPF should be on the minimal side. \nBy Sunday though, moisture will be hitting its peak in our CWA. \nAs guidance stands there is little confidence in the exact \ntrajectory of the broad moisture plume. If we are to lean one \nway or another, more evidence is suggesting that the strongest \nportion of the plume will pass to our south, though even in this\nscenario, southwestern Colorado is in line for yet another wet \nstretch with PWAT's up to 300% of normal. Adding to the \ncomplexity of this event is the progression of the upper-level \npattern. As the PacNW low propagates eastwards towards the \nnorthern Rockies, a second low develops at the base of the \ntrough, swinging through the southwestern US before eventually \nrejoining the parent trough early in the work week. The movement\nof both lows would likely have significant impacts on the \nnature of this storm system as temperatures will plummet to \naround 10 degrees below normal with their passage. This could be\nenough to drop snow level 1-2 kft. The complex pattern continues\nto lead to low confidence in the timing and location of any \neventual snowfall though, and as such we cannot yet speculate on\nhow impacts will manifest from said snowfall. What we can say \nnow though is that any notable amount of precipitation over \nsouthwestern Colorado locations recovering from last week's \nrainfall will have the potential to exacerbate flooding \nconcerns. We are keeping a very close eye on this next round of \nprecipitation as a result, and over the coming days as more \ndetails come into view we will begin to better gauge the risks \nSunday into Monday morning. As such, stay tuned for additional \ninformation throughout this week.",
